WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:07.337
You know, one of my earliest memories

00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:10.006
as a kid growing up in Jordan

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:11.883
was watching my father,

00:00:11.925 --> 00:00:14.094
who was a government engineer at the time,

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:15.512
set up a new computer.

00:00:16.388 --> 00:00:21.184
I was just a kid,
but that machine really gripped me.

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:23.395
It felt like it was magic.

00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:26.106
We couldn't really afford it,
but my father had a feeling

00:00:26.106 --> 00:00:28.858
that this thing
is going to be transformational

00:00:28.858 --> 00:00:30.986
for the world and for our lives.

00:00:31.653 --> 00:00:35.240
At first, like most kids,
I started playing video games.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.952
I figured out how to boot up video games
and play them for hours.

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:40.954
But then I had a radical thought.

00:00:40.954 --> 00:00:42.914
What if I could make my own video games?

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.128
The very notion that I could
take a concept in my head

00:00:48.169 --> 00:00:53.425
and make it a reality
that I and other people can explore

00:00:53.466 --> 00:00:56.094
was something that was really gripping.

00:00:56.136 --> 00:00:59.014
Now fast-forward to my teenage years

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:01.433
and the internet has just arrived.

00:01:01.474 --> 00:01:04.185
And I thought it was going
to change everything.

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:07.564
I was bursting with business ideas.

00:01:07.564 --> 00:01:08.940
But there was a problem.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.400
I didn't know where to start.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.235
Which language do you use?

00:01:12.235 --> 00:01:15.238
How do you even share
your creations with the world?

00:01:15.238 --> 00:01:17.907
And these questions
sent me down a rabbit hole.

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:23.038
The first thing I attempted doing
at solving these problems

00:01:23.038 --> 00:01:26.041
is putting a code editor in the browser.

00:01:26.082 --> 00:01:30.462
Can you make it so that you can
code anything really easily

00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:32.130
and share it with the world?

00:01:32.172 --> 00:01:33.882
So eventually I had a breakthrough.

00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:37.844
I built the world's first
online programming sandbox

00:01:37.886 --> 00:01:39.554
with a lot of different languages,

00:01:39.596 --> 00:01:41.264
and that went viral.

00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:44.601
A bunch of companies
in Silicon Valley started using it,

00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:45.894
and they hired me.

00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:49.856
I got a visa to the United States
and I came to New York.

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:55.236
A few years later, I set eyes to go west

00:01:55.236 --> 00:01:57.906
and I came to Silicon Valley
to start a company.

00:01:57.906 --> 00:01:59.157
And that was Replit.

00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:05.580
Our dream is to make it so that anyone
can turn an idea into software quickly.

00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:10.877
Our vision is to empower
a billion software creators.

00:02:12.671 --> 00:02:16.132
But giving people an editor
was not going to be enough

00:02:16.174 --> 00:02:18.843
to get a billion people coding on it.

00:02:18.885 --> 00:02:20.553
We've signed up millions of people

00:02:20.595 --> 00:02:23.890
and today we have 25 million developers
registered on our site.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:27.727
But we need an order of magnitude jump.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:31.356
And a couple of years ago
I stumbled on this technology.

00:02:31.356 --> 00:02:33.441
That's the reason we're all here.

00:02:33.441 --> 00:02:35.402
Large language models.

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:38.321
And I thought this was the unlock

00:02:38.321 --> 00:02:42.283
that's going to make it possible
for anyone to become a software creator.

00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:45.578
The interesting thing
about large language models

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:48.873
is that you can transform
any thought in any language,

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:50.875
any human language,

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:52.168
into software.

00:02:53.628 --> 00:02:57.549
And this, I thought, is how we bring
a billion software creators online.

00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:00.969
So right now I want you
to imagine something.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.221
Let's say you're traveling in a new city.

00:03:04.431 --> 00:03:08.935
And you're walking around
and seeing these magnificent landmarks.

00:03:10.311 --> 00:03:13.982
But you're interested in the stories
around these landmarks.

00:03:13.982 --> 00:03:17.068
Now it kind of takes you out of the moment
to pull up your phone

00:03:17.068 --> 00:03:21.114
and Google and find a Wikipedia page
for that particular landmark.

00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:25.326
Wouldn't it be easier if you had a map

00:03:25.326 --> 00:03:28.621
with the Wikipedia links
superimposed on the map?

00:03:28.621 --> 00:03:33.042
Typically, an app idea like that
will be just a fleeting thought,

00:03:33.084 --> 00:03:36.963
and maybe you just forget about it
and continue with your life.

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:38.673
Maybe you'll buy a domain.

00:03:39.758 --> 00:03:42.260
And I know I've been guilty
of buying tons of domains

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:44.512
that never turn into anything.

00:03:44.554 --> 00:03:47.766
But in the world that I'm envisioning,

00:03:47.807 --> 00:03:50.935
you could actually do something
about it in that moment.

00:03:50.977 --> 00:03:54.439
You pull out your phone
and you talk to your AI assistant.

00:03:55.732 --> 00:03:59.652
And the AI assistant
is not just a passive listener.

00:03:59.652 --> 00:04:01.863
It's going to start
brainstorming with you.

00:04:01.863 --> 00:04:03.406
It's going to make connections.

00:04:03.406 --> 00:04:06.242
It'll find projects
you've worked on in the past

00:04:06.242 --> 00:04:08.203
that kind of look like this idea.

00:04:09.537 --> 00:04:11.873
And let's say you want
to give a clearer picture.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:14.584
Well, pull out a napkin. Draw a mark.

00:04:14.626 --> 00:04:16.002
Snap a picture

00:04:16.044 --> 00:04:18.713
and boom, you send it to your agent.

00:04:20.131 --> 00:04:23.218
Let's start strategizing
and thinking of a road map.

00:04:23.259 --> 00:04:26.513
Now you don't have a lot of time
to chat with it, you're on the go.

00:04:26.554 --> 00:04:28.264
So you send it a voice note.

00:04:30.183 --> 00:04:31.434
Now you head home.

00:04:34.187 --> 00:04:35.563
And you have a prototype.

00:04:35.563 --> 00:04:38.650
You have a tangible thing
that you could actually play with.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.778
You have a starting point
to make an application out of.

00:04:42.904 --> 00:04:46.741
But it's not just a prototype.
You also have a plan.

00:04:46.741 --> 00:04:49.786
You have a plan of action
that could turn this prototype

00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:52.413
into a production app
that you can share with the world.

00:04:54.249 --> 00:04:57.210
Now, apps take time and they take money.

00:04:57.252 --> 00:05:00.505
So we're going to set a budget
of how much money we're going to spend

00:05:00.547 --> 00:05:02.966
and how much time
we're going to spend on this app.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.137
To take a look behind the scenes,

00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:11.516
it's not just magic,
although it'll feel magical.

00:05:11.558 --> 00:05:14.602
The plan editor becomes a plan source.

00:05:14.644 --> 00:05:18.064
It recursively expands
all the different tasks.

00:05:18.064 --> 00:05:20.108
It goes out and assembles the tools.

00:05:20.108 --> 00:05:23.319
And these are tools that,
as developers, we use every day.

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:26.114
There's your code editor,
your source control

00:05:26.114 --> 00:05:27.740
and everything that we're used to.

00:05:28.741 --> 00:05:30.368
And you have, of course, the code.

00:05:32.036 --> 00:05:33.872
Now this is not just about coding.

00:05:33.872 --> 00:05:37.041
It's about a new way
of communicating with technology.

00:05:37.083 --> 00:05:42.088
Being able to talk and draw
and work with AI

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.507
to make your visions reality.

00:05:45.174 --> 00:05:47.510
Let's go back to our plan.

00:05:47.552 --> 00:05:49.178
We're going to run it.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.639
It has four feature ideas.

00:05:51.681 --> 00:05:54.684
The first one is user auth.

00:05:54.726 --> 00:05:56.185
Every app needs that.

00:05:56.936 --> 00:06:01.733
The second one is a way
to collect feedback from users.

00:06:01.733 --> 00:06:05.111
And the third one
is a way to like places you've been to.

00:06:05.111 --> 00:06:07.155
And the fourth one is a navigation.

00:06:08.364 --> 00:06:10.575
The first branch is kind of easy.

00:06:10.575 --> 00:06:15.079
The AI has seen login pages
a trillion times and knows how to code it.

00:06:15.079 --> 00:06:17.749
We'll take a look at the code.
We'll merge it.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.294
The second one is a little bit hard.
Maybe it's a novel task.

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:23.713
But the AI here is prompting us

00:06:23.755 --> 00:06:25.632
to add more compute.

00:06:26.424 --> 00:06:28.927
So the easiest way to solve this problem

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:31.012
would be to pump in more compute.

00:06:31.054 --> 00:06:32.805
Maybe that means a larger model,

00:06:32.847 --> 00:06:34.891
or additional contacts
or additional tokens

00:06:34.933 --> 00:06:36.434
in order to solve this problem.

00:06:37.352 --> 00:06:38.895
And the problem is solved.

00:06:38.937 --> 00:06:40.521
The third branch is kind of easy.

00:06:40.521 --> 00:06:42.190
Smooth sailing.

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:45.151
I look at it, it works, I merge it.

00:06:46.444 --> 00:06:48.947
Now the fourth one is kind of difficult.

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:52.283
The AI seems confident

00:06:52.283 --> 00:06:56.204
that it's not going to be able
to solve this problem with more compute.

00:06:56.204 --> 00:06:58.247
So it prompts me with an increased budget.

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:00.208
Now it's a hefty number,

00:07:00.249 --> 00:07:03.461
and that's because
it's not just going to recruit AIs.

00:07:03.503 --> 00:07:06.923
It's going to go out and recruit people.
It's going to post a bounty.

00:07:07.548 --> 00:07:09.926
So in this case,
we need a human in the loop.

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:11.886
And until we get AGI,

00:07:11.928 --> 00:07:15.431
today, natural intelligence
is going to be really, really important.

00:07:16.975 --> 00:07:19.477
So we go out, we get a human.

00:07:20.311 --> 00:07:23.523
That quota is going to
also be assisted by AI,

00:07:23.523 --> 00:07:26.359
and they're going to help me
solve the problem.

00:07:26.359 --> 00:07:28.152
I merge everything together

00:07:28.152 --> 00:07:33.908
and it's just a matter of hours
and 50-60 dollars or so,

00:07:33.908 --> 00:07:35.493
I have an app I can deploy.

00:07:35.493 --> 00:07:38.454
I deploy it, I share it with the world.

00:07:38.454 --> 00:07:40.206
Now to step back a little bit,

00:07:40.248 --> 00:07:43.584
notice that I didn't write
a single line of code.

00:07:43.626 --> 00:07:47.046
I was basically the creative
heartbeat of the project.

00:07:47.088 --> 00:07:48.339
I manage the project,

00:07:48.381 --> 00:07:50.508
I manage both AI and people,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.469
mediated by AI

00:07:53.511 --> 00:07:56.055
in order to build this application.

00:07:56.097 --> 00:08:01.811
And that fleeting thought that you had
is suddenly an application.

00:08:02.562 --> 00:08:06.899
So, you know, I can almost hear
some of you thinking, like,

00:08:06.899 --> 00:08:09.193
"OK, Amjad, this is this is great and all.

00:08:09.193 --> 00:08:11.154
This is a fascinating vision

00:08:11.154 --> 00:08:14.782
but it kind of looks
too fantastic, two futuristic."

00:08:14.782 --> 00:08:18.161
Well, I'm here to tell you
it's kind of already happening.

00:08:18.161 --> 00:08:23.207
So just a couple of weeks ago,
an entrepreneur on Replit, Yoeri --

00:08:23.249 --> 00:08:26.586
he's a technical recruiter by training,
he's not a software engineer --

00:08:26.627 --> 00:08:29.130
he learned enough code
to start building his startup.

00:08:29.172 --> 00:08:32.884
And thousands of miles
across the world we have Akashdeep.

00:08:32.925 --> 00:08:36.929
Akashdeep, a student in India,
he comes from a farming family

00:08:36.971 --> 00:08:38.389
and he can't afford a PC.

00:08:38.431 --> 00:08:41.809
He has never had a PC.
All he's had was his Android phone.

00:08:41.851 --> 00:08:46.147
He started learning to code in Replit
just a few months ago,

00:08:46.147 --> 00:08:50.485
and then decided to go
and start making money on the platform.

00:08:50.485 --> 00:08:54.280
Now he makes more money
than his entire family

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.158
and he can afford a lot
of different computers, not just one.

00:08:57.158 --> 00:09:01.746
And now he's joined forces
with Yoeri to build that startup.

00:09:01.746 --> 00:09:04.832
You know, today we talk
about the Fortune 500,

00:09:04.832 --> 00:09:07.919
and it takes a ton of employees
to build a big company like that.

00:09:08.836 --> 00:09:11.047
But I think in the future,

00:09:11.089 --> 00:09:15.718
when AI is helping everyone
build their dreams,

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.596
we’re going to be talking
about the Fortune 5000000.

00:09:18.638 --> 00:09:20.515
And again, this is not just --

00:09:21.849 --> 00:09:23.810
This is not just a fantasy.

00:09:23.851 --> 00:09:25.686
We're already building this.

00:09:25.728 --> 00:09:30.650
Last week we announced
that we're open-sourcing our AI models,

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:34.028
and we're making it free for all our users

00:09:34.028 --> 00:09:39.242
because we think that's a massive step up
for all of humanity.

00:09:39.242 --> 00:09:43.579
AI is not just a tool, it's a ladder
that equalizes opportunity.

00:09:43.579 --> 00:09:48.417
And that opens up horizons for people
that are typically on the sidelines.

00:09:48.459 --> 00:09:50.419
So I'll leave you with this.

00:09:50.461 --> 00:09:52.130
Imagine that

00:09:52.171 --> 00:09:54.465
the American dream,

00:09:54.507 --> 00:09:56.134
not just --

00:09:56.175 --> 00:09:59.595
not just in one place,

00:09:59.637 --> 00:10:01.222
but actually in the cloud.

00:10:02.682 --> 00:10:03.933
Thank you.

00:10:03.975 --> 00:10:05.560
(Applause)

